  "tagline": "Buddhist summer ancestor-honoring folk dance, parent of regional Bon dances",
  "profile": "Bon Odori ('Bon dance') is the umbrella style of folk dancing performed during the Buddhist Obon festival to welcome and honour the spirits of ancestors, with a history of nearly 600 years originating in the Nenbutsu odori (Wikipedia, Obon). A typical Bon dance has people circling a raised wooden scaffold (yagura) in yukata, accompanied by regional min'yo folk songs - so the dance looks and sounds different region to region (e.g. Soran Bushi in Hokkaido, Gujo Odori in Gifu, Awa Odori in Tokushima) (Nippon.com).",
  "insight": "Per Wikipedia, Bon Odori originates from the story of Maha Maudgalyayana (Mokuren), a disciple of the Buddha who danced with joy upon freeing his mother's spirit - making it fundamentally a dance of gratitude to ancestors.",
